Where they came from
--------------------

U35, U33, U34, U13 and U26 are MAME's "ceci" ROM set (driver
src/mame/apple/apple2e.cpp), file names kept as MAME has them so that
they can be checked against it; each matches the CRC32 and SHA-1 in
that driver. Checksums of every page of the address space, taken on
the working machine with a short program, match them as the machine
reads them.

U7 is MAME's version 1.1 system ROM (u7.tmm24256ap.bin, SHA-256
18bcd19e81a373efc973843429358b41346639900530cae467e42e7ee9dd13ef) with
21 bytes changed, so that it reproduces every page and block checksum
measured on the working machine. The four pages that differ were
listed with the machine's own monitor. The changes (CPU address: MAME's
bytes -> the machine's):

  $D300-$D310  AD 63 C0 10 08 A9 00 8D F4 03 8D 7B 04 20 84 FE 60
               -> FF x 17          (a reset hook, absent on the machine)
  $FA64-$FA65  00 D3 -> 84 FE      (the reset: JSR $D300 -> JSR $FE84)
  $C86D        08 -> EA            (the disk boot search: PHP -> NOP)
  $CDFC        6F -> 34            (the self-test's check value for
                                    $D000-$FFFF, to match)

The machine boots, runs its self-test (every line OK) and behaves as
the working one does on this image.
